    "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function",
    "problem": "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$",
    "worked_solution": "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{|}_{(-6,-\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
